   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT establishing the 400 Years of African-American History Commission; and\nproviding for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S3204",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3204-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 21,
       "fullName": "Kevin S. Parker",
       "imgName": "416_kevin_s._parker.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 416,
       "sessionMemberId": 1194,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "PARKER"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Arts and Cultural Affairs",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3204\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act establishing the 400 Years of African-American History Commis-\nsion; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration\nthereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill shall be to highlight the contributions that\nAfricans and African-Americans have made to our country and to our\nstate. In addition, it shall be to bring communities together under\nevents, activities and educational research by the 400 year commission\nand partnered community based organizations. The events and activities\ncarried out by the commission can foster learning and developmental\nenvironments for youth, adults and seniors of all backgrounds and culti-\nvate opportunities to foster a better understanding of the development\nof our country, which was and is possible through the continuous\ncontributions of African-Americans from 1619 until present day.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\n1) Section 1 shall establish the commission.\n\n2) Section 2 shall define the criteria for membership, and filling\nvacancies of the commission.\n\n3) Section 3 shall define the responsibilities of the commission as they\nrelate to education, coordination, commemoration, research and all other\nduties of the commission defined in that section.\n\n4) Section 4 defines ways in which the commission shall use appropriated\nfunds.\n\n5) Section 5 establishes the contents of the first report by the commis-\nsion before any events, activities or expenditure of funds.\n\n6) Section 6 is the second report, which will summarize all activities\nand final accounting of all expenditures as well as all research find-\nings and recommendations.\n\n7) Section 7 contains the effective date and a date in which this act\nshall be deemed repealed.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nUnder current law, New York has not established a 400 years of African-\nAmerican History Commission.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe contributions that African Americans have made to our society are\noften overlooked and it is damaging to our diverse multidultural socie-\nty, which was built on the contributions of African Americans from the\ncolonial era, the early years of nation, to the industrial and techno-\nlogical revolutions within our great country and state. It is even more\nimportant in a state as diverse as our own that we come together on this\nmonumental anniversary and celebrate the contributions African Americans\nhave made in the 400 years since they first arrived on the shores of the\neast coast.\n\nThe idea that it is necessary stems from the fact that African American\ncontributions to our society are great and expansive and as such, we\nshould establish a commission with a purpose to highlight and integrate\nall of the many great contributions of African Americans to our state\nand country. In addition, that it should be done in ways in which,\nentire communities can unify and commemorate an anniversary such as this\none.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill: Senate Bill 8076.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal, as all members of the commission shall severe without compen-\nsation but shall be entitled to reimbursement for all necessary\nexpenses.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall expire and be deemed\nrepealed July 1, 2021.\n",
